Tim, thanks for filling in and adding much useful information.
I'll be the first to admit I'm a layman when it comes to nukes and
weapons of mass destruction, but some of Tims post on nukes ignore
certain alternatives and leave holes.  A year or two ago, I managed to 
get into a one time class on weapons of mass destruction.  The 
course materials included general descriptions of how weapons of 
mass destruction were made and used, and the current problems surrounding
their use.  Being only an introductory class, I'll can't claim any
authority, but you can check it out yourself.  

The best, and most user-friendly book in the course was, I believe,
called _Doom's Day Weapons in the Hands of Many_  I'm not sure who 
the author was, but since it is one of the few books I brought with
me this year (to Germany), I'll check it out and post it to the list.


Anyways, there are a few of Tim's points I would like to mention:
 
} Garrett Goebel writes:
} >Anyone can make a nuclear bomb.  The hard part is getting the high grade
} >uranium or plutonium.  All you have to do is incase the uranium/plutonium
} >and set up a small explosive charge that will force all the radioactive
} >material close together... like a grenade that explodes inward.
} 
} Not "anyone." I was a physicist and I certainly don't think I could build
} even a crude bomb with a reasonable chance of it working. [stuff deleted]
