} mass down a massive pipe from 2-3 floors up (inertial driver, with a few
} hundred pounds of concrete or steel behind the falling mass) and into the
} other subcritical piece., sort of like the original "Little Boy" uranium
} bomb. Stuff I've learned more recently suggests this wouldn't work
} (nonuniformity of the explosion). In any case, having no test facilities
} would be a severe crimp.

I'd heard of this one too... but I believe uniformity of explosion isn't
that hard to get (only 2 physics classes in high school so don't trust me).
Take two metal mixing bowls cut them so that when placed together they form
a sphere.  Place charges of your favorite nitrate explosives, or plastique
if you know how to make, it uniformly around the sphere.  Wire the explosives
to a clock.  Encase the sphere in a stronger casing to insure that the blast
is inward... and make sure you stand back a couple miles when testing.

I would think that even if the charges didn't go off uniformly, encasing
the uranium inside two spherical shells, the outmost being the thickest,
would insure that pressure would be applied uniformly to the radioactive
material.  Correct me if I'm wrong.

The hard part, as I'm sure Tim and I would agree, is getting the high grade
uranium.  I guess the wiring would take looking at an introductory electronics
manual for 15 minutes... And mixing the explosives for the charges... would
require either a wallet, or some knowledge about explosives.
 
