Followup-To: solovay@netcom.com

This is a call for votes to create a new unmoderated group,
rec.arts.books.tolkien. (The name was determined by a straw poll.)
This post contains information about the proposed group and voting
instructions.

NEWSGROUP

  rec.arts.books.tolkien (unmoderated)

VOTING

  The voting period will begin when this CFV is posted. It will end at
  23:59:59 PST March 19, 1993. Votes must be *received* by the
  deadline. According to the USENET rules, in order for the group to
  be created, at least 2/3 of the votes cast must be "YES" votes, and
  there must be at least 100 more "YES" votes than "NO" votes.

  Votes must be sent to one of the following two addresses:

        solovay@netcom.com
	bsw!asolovay@uunet.uu.net

  You may simply "reply" to this posting. Each address can accept both
  "YES" and "NO" votes. Votes posted to any newsgroup will NOT be
  counted.

  The subject line should be an unambiguous "yes" or "no" vote (e.g.
  "I vote {YES,NO} on the creation of rec.arts.books.tolkien as
  proposed"). If the subject line doesn't have such a statement, I
  will check the body of the message for the vote.

  Please include your full name.

  Conditional statements (e.g. "I would vote YES if...") will be
  considered comments, not votes. If you wish to make comments, you
  are welcome to do so; however, please make sure that your ballot
  begins with an unambiguous vote.

  No more than one vote may come from any one account; you may not
  mail votes on behalf of another person. Each person is permitted to
  vote only once, no matter how many accounts he or she might have. If
  a single person or account sends more than one vote, the vote with
  the latest "sending" time will be the official vote.

  I will attempt to acknowledge every vote via EMail (i.e. "Your NO
  vote against the creation of rec.arts.books.tolkien has been
  received").  There will also be two repeats of this CFV, each of
  which will have a full list of voters (without specifying which way
  they voted).

  If you are unsure whether your vote was received, you may EMail me
  at either of the above addresses. However, I cannot divulge the vote
  tallies until the end of the voting period. I also cannot answer
  questions about the content of the group (other than by mailing a
  copy of the charter).

============================================================

CHARTER 

  rec.arts.books.tolkien (hereinafter referred to as "The Group")
  would be a forum for discussion of all the works of Professor J.R.R.
  Tolkien (hereinafter "JRRT"). It would be open to discussion of his
  "Middle-earth" works, his scholarly writings, his essays and poetry,
  and all other works of JRRT (of whatever nature). The Group would be
  open to discussion at any level; for example, discussion of his
  "Middle-Earth" works could vary from literary criticism to
  philological discussion of the book's languages, or any other topic
  bearing on the works.

  The Group would also be open for discussion of works by other
  authors which relate to JRRT's work (including, but not limited to,
  scholarly criticism of his work, supplemental and speculative
  writings about his worlds, analyses of the various languages created
  by JRRT, and (of course) the elaborate historical material published
  by Christopher Tolkien). The group would be open to discussion about
  art works which are based on Tolkien's works (e.g. graphic
  depictions of scenes from his worlds, musical settings of his
  ballads and poetry).  The group would furthermore be open to
  discussion about JRRT the man, and how the circumstances of his life
  relate to his writing.

  The Group would be open to comparisons of Tolkien's works with those
  of other authors, e.g. comparing the influence of Christianity
  JRRT's writing with its influence on C.S. Lewis's works; comparing
  Sauron with Milton's Satan as a figure of evil; etc.  If such a
  discussion drifted into a discussion of the other author alone (and
  no longer touched on Tolkien), it would no longer be appropriate for
  the Group, and participants would be asked to move the discussion to
  a more appropriate newsgroup, or to EMail.

  Fan fiction would be permitted, provided that it did not violate
  anyone's rights or copyrights (especially the rights and copyrights
  of the Tolkien estate). Posters of fan fiction would be strongly
  encouraged to put the identifying word "FANFIC" in the "subject"
  line (to simplify kill-filing). Satire and parody of Tolkien's work
  would also be permitted. If the poster could reasonably expect
  readers to be offended by the posting, he or she should post it in
  rot-13 encryption (i.e. each letter is mapped to the letter 13
  places on in the alphabet, so that 'a' becomes 'n', 'b' becomes 'o',
  etc.).

  Willful and/or repeated spelling of JRRT's name as "Tolkein" would
  be punished by brutal sarcasm.


