I want to live in a society where everyone can pack a gun. I cannot
morally impose gun ownership on people, so I suspect many people will
choose to be unarmed. I would not, however, be dismayed by a large fraction
of the population being armed. I would like to be armed. My ex-girlfriend
whom I still care about is a slightly built individual who suffers constant
harassment on the streets by virtue of being good looking and female and
who has been attacked in the past -- I would feel much better knowing she
could kill an attacker, and she's not going to be able to do it without
weapons.

> Do you really want to live in a society that allows anyone to
> amass as much weaponry as they choose?

Yes. I suspect that amassing too much weaponry (such as someone building
nuclear weapons in their basement) might lead to a revokation of
one's protection contract and bad consequences, but in general, yes,
I want most people to be able to get as many guns as they can afford
if they so choose. Remeber, by the way, that most people can only manage
to fire one gun at a time. Those who can manage two are rare -- more
than that is impossible.

> I don't.

This is an area of disagreement. However, lets be honest for a moment. SOMEONE
is going to have weapons. Do you want it to be just criminals? Do you
want it to be the state, which is an organization that historically has
behaved in quite a criminal manner? It is NOT a coincidence that the
Nazis disarmed the populace soon after gaining power -- statists need
people unarmed.

> exactly. and is the reason that such a conclusion *must* be avoided. 
> society, Libertarian or statist, has to have power to deal with 
> those who would use their freedom to wrest power for themselves at the expense
